# spi-panel — a minimal SoC-shaped board with an SPI display.
#
#   rsemu run spi-panel --media firmware=fw.bin
#   rsemu run spi-panel --media firmware=fw.bin -p link=transactional
#
# The smallest machine that exercises a whole display path: a hart, some RAM
# holding a framebuffer, an SPI controller, a Sitronix ST7272A panel on the far
# end of it, and a scanout engine that reads the framebuffer and hands a host a
# picture. It models no product. It exists so that the SPI bus and the display
# devices have somewhere real to run, and so that `rsemu run` can draw something.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The one decision this file is written to make explicit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `docs/buses/low-speed.md` asks for it by name: SPI is a timing protocol on
# wires, most emulators model it transactionally, that is much faster and
# usually fine — and some guest firmware bit-bangs the lines through GPIO and
# will notice. So the choice is a parameter, and the default is written down
# rather than inherited.
#
#   link = "wired"          the default here. The controller drives SCK, MOSI
#                           and CS as real wires, one edge per half bit period,
#                           paced by the scheduler. Slower, and what firmware
#                           that also pokes the pins itself requires. This board
#                           defaults to it because it is the path most worth
#                           having under test.
#
#   link = "transactional"  one call per sixteen-bit word straight to the panel,
#                           no wires moved. The ordinary fast model.
#
# **A transfer costs the same virtual time either way** — `bits x 2 x (CLKDIV+1)`
# ticks of `sysclk` — so firmware that polls the controller's BUSY flag sees the
# same timing under both. What differs is only whether the individual edges
# exist for anything else to watch.
#
# Both attachments are declared below, because a `.machine` file is static and a
# `param` chooses a property rather than whether a statement exists. Whichever
# the controller is not using simply sits idle: in `transactional` the wires
# rest at their idle levels, and in `wired` nothing ever drives the named bus.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is modelled, and what is not
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The **ST7272A's SPI carries registers only** — brightness, contrast, gamma,
# scan direction, power sequencing. It holds no framebuffer; its datasheet's
# block diagram (§5) has no frame memory in it and its whole register map (§8.1)
# has no memory-write command. Pixels reach a real panel over a separate
# parallel-RGB link. `src/dev/sitronix/st7272a.rs` sets out that evidence.
#
# That link is **not modelled**, deliberately: no guest can observe a pixel
# clock, a DE edge or an HSYNC. So `lcd.scanout` reads the framebuffer straight
# out of `mem` and presents it, and "parallel RGB" is a geometry, a pixel format
# and a frame period rather than a bus. See `src/dev/lcd/scanout.rs`.
#
# The two devices are consequently **independent today**: the panel records its
# parameters and can apply them to a pixel, but nothing hands it the scanout
# engine's pixels, because one device reaching another's typed handle is a
# `core::device` seam that does not exist yet. When it lands, the engine calls
# `St7272a::apply` and a firmware that dims the panel over SPI produces a dimmer
# PNG. Until then, writing brightness is checked by the panel's own tests and is
# not visible in the picture, and this file would rather say so.

machine "spi-panel" {
  # Which modelling style the SPI link uses. See the note above; this is the
  # decision `docs/buses/low-speed.md` asks not to make by accident.
  param link = "wired"

  # Which named SPI bus the controller and the panel meet on, so that two of
  # these can run in one process without talking over each other
  # (`-p spibus=b`). The same reason `riscv-virt` parameterises its console: the
  # rendezvous table in `bus::spi::buses` is process-wide until `core::bus`
  # exists.
  param spibus = "spi0"

  # How much RAM the board has. The framebuffer lives at the bottom of it.
  param ram = 4M

  # Where the framebuffer starts. A property rather than a constant, because a
  # guest normally programs the controller's BASE register and this is only the
  # value it comes out of reset with.
  param fb = 0x10000000

  # The panel's geometry. The ST7272A of the datasheet is 320RGB x 240 (§2), and
  # a board pairing the driver with another panel changes it here.
  param width  = 320
  param height = 240

  # -- crystals --------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Two, because the board has two: a system clock the hart and the SPI
  # controller run from, and a pixel clock for the display. They are separate
  # oscillator trees, which is the physical truth — a display dot clock is its
  # own can, and no correct program can depend on its phase against the CPU
  # (ROADMAP.md §4.2).
  osc sysclk = 100000000 Hz

  # 6 MHz is the ST7272A's *typical* DCLK for the parallel 24-bit interface
  # (datasheet §7.3.4: Fclk min 5, typ 6, max 8 MHz). With the same table's
  # typical periods — Th = 371 DCLK, Tv = 260 HSYNC — one frame is 96,460 ticks,
  # which is 16.077 ms, or 62.2 frames a second. Not 60: the real number, from
  # the real table, arrived at by exact integer arithmetic.
  osc dclk = 6000000 Hz

  # -- the bus ---------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # One flat 32-bit space. `unassigned` is left at its default of `fault`, so an
  # access to a hole is an access fault the guest can see rather than a
  # plausible-looking zero — which hides our memory-map bugs as well as the
  # guest's.
  space mem { width = 32 }

  # -- the hart --------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # RV32IMA, which is enough to run a firmware image and nothing more. There is
  # no interrupt controller on this board and nothing raises one: the display
  # path here is entirely polled.
  object cpu "cpu.riscv" {
    clock  = sysclk
    space  = mem
    engine = "interp"
    xlen   = "rv32"
    isa    = "ima"
    hartid = 0
    reset  = 0x00000000
  }

  # -- memory ----------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # 64 KiB of firmware ROM at zero, which is where the hart starts. `image`
  # names a media slot: whoever runs the machine binds the bytes
  # (`--media firmware=fw.bin`), and nothing below host/ opens a file.
  object fw "rom" { size = 64K, image = "firmware" }

  object dram "ram" { size = ram }

  # -- the SPI controller ----------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Clocked from the system clock, so a transfer's duration is counted in the
  # same ticks the hart's instructions are. One chip select, for the panel.
  object spi "spi.controller" {
    clock        = sysclk
    link         = link
    bus          = spibus
    chip-selects = 1
  }

  # -- the panel -------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # On the pixel clock, because its one piece of timed behaviour is the command
  # latch: datasheet §7.1(c), "commands are established by the VSYNC signal", so
  # a write lands in a shadow bank and the panel adopts it at the next frame
  # boundary. `htotal` and `vtotal` are §7.3.4's typicals, and their product is
  # where that boundary falls.
  object panel "sitronix.st7272a" {
    clock  = dclk
    width  = width
    height = height
    htotal = 371
    vtotal = 260
    bus    = spibus
    cs     = 0
  }

  # -- the display controller ------------------------------------------------
  #
  # A bus master: it reads the framebuffer out of `mem` itself, so it declares
  # the space DMA traverses (ROADMAP.md §4.4). Same clock and same totals as the
  # panel, because on a real board the controller *is* what generates the
  # panel's pixel clock and sync — it is the same timing seen from the other end.
  #
  # This register block is rsemu's own generic one, not any SoC's. A specific
  # SoC's display controller is a thin wrapper over the same engine.
  object lcdc "lcd.scanout" {
    clock  = dclk
    space  = mem
    width  = width
    height = height
    htotal = 371
    vtotal = 260
    base   = fb
    format = "rgb888"
  }

  # -- the memory map --------------------------------------------------------
  map mem 0x00000000 size 64K  = fw     # reset vector and firmware
  map mem 0x10000000 size ram  = dram   # RAM, framebuffer at the bottom
  # Both register blocks are smaller than their window and neither decodes the
  # address bits above its own, so each answers over and over through it — which
  # is what a peripheral on a coarsely decoded SoC bus does, and what `mirror()`
  # says.
  map mem 0xf0000000 size 4K   = mirror(spi)   # 6 registers, 170 times over
  map mem 0xf0001000 size 4K   = mirror(lcdc)  # 8 registers, 128 times over

  # -- the SPI wires ---------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Used when `link = "wired"`, idle otherwise. `SDA` is one bidirectional pin
  # on the real part; it is split into `mosi` and `miso` here because an rsemu
  # wire has fixed drivers and cannot be tri-stated, and a 3-wire controller
  # ties the two together outside the chip.
  wire spi.sck    -> panel.sck
  wire spi.mosi   -> panel.mosi
  wire spi.cs0    -> panel.cs
  wire panel.miso -> spi.miso

  # Not wired, and deliberately: the panel's `grb`, `disp` and `bist_en` pins.
  # This board ties GRB and DISP high and BIST_EN low, which is what an unwired
  # control pin already means (see `src/dev/sitronix/st7272a.rs`), so naming
  # them here would add three nets that never change. Firmware turns the display
  # on through register 10h instead, which is the interesting path.
}
